# Text files are LF in the working tree on every operating system. This repo's whole claim is that
# the same bytes produce the same page everywhere, and a checkout that rewrites line endings breaks
# that claim in two measured places: a test comparing a generated string (built partly from raw
# string literals in a .cs file) against a file on disk, and the CSV hazard fixture below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF endings: the release workflow runs build/macos/make-app-bundle.sh
# on a macOS runner, where a CRLF shebang line fails with "bad interpreter".
*.sh text eol=lf

# The roster import fixtures are byte-exact hazard cases, not ordinary text. members-hazards.csv
# carries a CRLF inside a quoted field — that IS the hazard under test, and normalising it on
# checkout deletes the thing the test is checking. Left as text, the assertion passed on the
# machine that authored the file and failed on every CI runner.
tests/Roster.Tests/Fixtures/*.csv -text

# Binaries git should never inspect or transform. Snapshot baselines and font files in particular:
# a single byte changed is a failed test, which is the point of them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ttf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.tboard binary
*.pdf binary
